WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.179
The file you write once and the agent reads every time.

2
00:00:03.459 --> 00:00:08.813
Every coding agent starts a session knowing nothing about your project except what it can read.

3
00:00:09.093 --> 00:00:15.472
Project memory is how you hand over the rest: a Markdown file the harness loads at the start of each session.

4
00:00:15.752 --> 00:00:17.224
In Claude Code it’s CLAUDE.

5
00:00:17.504 --> 00:00:21.621
md; in Codex, Copilot’s agents, and many others it’s AGENTS.

6
00:00:21.901 --> 00:00:24.504
md; in Gemini CLI it’s GEMINI.

7
00:00:24.784 --> 00:00:25.595
md.

8
00:00:25.875 --> 00:00:29.224
The idea is the same everywhere, and so are the mistakes.

9
00:00:29.504 --> 00:00:38.549
The Claude Code memory docs make one point that reframes everything else: Claude treats these files as context, not enforced configuration.

10
00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:50.392
The content is delivered as a message after the system prompt, and Claude reads it and tries to follow it, with no guarantee of strict compliance, especially for vague or conflicting instructions.

11
00:00:50.672 --> 00:00:55.365
The more specific and concise the instructions, the more consistently they’re followed.

12
00:00:55.645 --> 00:01:01.640
So there are two jobs here: put the right things in the file, and write them in a way that changes behavior.

13
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.589
Anything that must happen every time goes elsewhere (lesson 5).
